## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cross-service flow with clear per-tenant boundaries, basic fault-tolerance, and observable behavior using Azure primitives.

## Key Concepts

- Azure Functions (HTTP triggers, Durable Functions)
- Cosmos DB (partition key design, per-tenant isolation)
- Azure Storage Queues (queue-based decoupling, per-tenant naming)
- Idempotency using eventId and a processed-tracker
- Retry policies and observability (Application Insights)

## Code Example

```typescript
// Lightweight enqueue example (pseudo)
import { QueueClient } from "@azure/storage-queue";
async function enqueueEvent(tenantId: string, eventId: string, payload: any) {
  const queue = new QueueClient("<connection-string>", `tenant-${tenantId}-events`);
  const body = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({ eventId, payload })).toString("base64");
  await queue.sendMessage(body);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test end-to-end with mocked webhook endpoints?
- How would you scale for higher event volumes while preserving at-least-once delivery and idempotency?

